<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    All logic lives here so the CLI and the MCP server can never disagree about what a
    query returns or how a node is written. The front ends bind arguments and shape
    envelopes; they do not decide anything.

    No framework dependencies on purpose: this project must be callable from a console
    app, a web host, and a test runner without dragging any of them in.
  -->

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The first package reference here, and the rule above is the reason it needed an
      argument. Azure.Identity is a library rather than a framework: it drags in no host,
      so a console app, a web host and a test runner can all still load this project.

      It is here rather than in the front ends because token acquisition is BEHAVIOUR:
      which scope alias resolves to which resource, and when a cached token is too near
      expiry to hand out. Behaviour that lives in a front end is behaviour the other front
      end gets to disagree about.

      Note for anyone introspecting it: on net10.0 this package ships a 17KB facade that
      forwards all 56 of its public types into Azure.Core. Reflection over the file itself
      reports zero types, which is true and reads as a broken lookup.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Azure.Identity" Version="1.21.0" />

    <!--
      The MCP bearer token's store (McpTokenStore), for the mcp-token and mcp-headers verbs and
      for ServerLink's Authorization header. Here rather than in Janet.Cli because the verbs
      live here, where Janet.Tests can reach them.

      The cost, stated because it bends the rule above: the package carries a FrameworkReference
      to Microsoft.AspNetCore.App (its guard and bearer check are middleware), and a framework
      reference flows to every consumer. So since 0.7.0 this project, and the janet CLI with it,
      needs the ASP.NET Core shared runtime, which any machine running janet-mcp already has.
      The alternative was a second token reader here, which is the drift the shared package
      exists to end.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Janet.Mcp.Security" Version="0.8.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- For WriteQueue.BeforeApplyForTests, the one seam that reaches the dead-consumer path. -->
    <InternalsVisibleTo Include="Janet.Tests" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
